[DOCUMENT]
TITLE: A slice is imperative work, and a use case is a claim about the same subject

[GRAMMAR]
IMPORT_FROM_FILE: @repo

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-SLICE-IS-WORK-NOT-A-USE-CASE
TITLE: A slice is imperative work, and a use case is a claim about the same subject
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
A SLICE is IMPERATIVE, so under DEC-NODE-TYPE-GRID it is not in the grid at all
-- it is work, alongside WORK itself. It is not a USE CASE, and the two are easy
to conflate because they are usually about the same subject.

The existing definition is a vertical unit of deliverable work that Crosses the
mechanisms it needs, and DEC-DEFER-LAYER names it as the one axis this graph can
currently express: the vertical, cutting the horizontal layers.

THE DIFFERENCE IS MOOD, not subject. A slice says BUILD THIS. A use case says
THIS PATH MUST WORK. One is an instruction and dies when it is carried out; the
other is a claim and outlives the work that satisfied it. So they pair rather
than compete -- a slice is frequently the work a use case motivates, and the use
case is what the slice is checked against afterwards.

The practical consequence for the migration: a slice does not become a use case,
and rewriting slices as use cases would silently convert a work queue into a set
of standing claims that nothing ever completes.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
One of the three types DEC-NODE-TYPE-GRID left unruled, and the one most likely to be mis-migrated, because a vertical slice and a use case describe the same territory from opposite moods. Recording the distinction before the migration keeps it from being decided by whoever edits first.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
The operator could not recall the definition on 2026-08-28 and reasoned from the name to 'a vertical slice through horizontal layers, so a use case'. The first half is exactly right and matches DEC-DEFER-LAYER; the second half is the conflation this node exists to prevent.
<<<
